1
00:00:06,770 --> 00:00:11,240
Two classes record how many minutes their journey to school takes.

2
00:00:11,290 --> 00:00:15,080
Two bar charts side by side are hard to compare.

3
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:21,020
What is a clearer way: two frequency polygons on one graph, or two pie charts far apart?

4
00:00:21,070 --> 00:00:24,240
Pause the video and choose.

5
00:00:24,290 --> 00:00:26,660
Two frequency polygons on one graph.

6
00:00:26,710 --> 00:00:29,430
You can compare them at a glance.

7
00:00:29,480 --> 00:00:35,090
Remember: the midpoint of the class from 10 up to 20 is 15.

8
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:42,000
A frequency polygon plots each class's frequency, then joins the points with straight lines.

9
00:00:42,050 --> 00:00:42,690
Your turn.

10
00:00:42,740 --> 00:00:47,360
Are the points plotted at the midpoints, or at the lower boundaries?

11
00:00:47,410 --> 00:00:50,620
Pause the video and choose.

12
00:00:50,670 --> 00:00:53,680
At the midpoints: 5, 15, 25 and 35.

13
00:00:53,730 --> 00:00:56,050
Class 9A is the solid line.

14
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:58,260
Class 9B is dashed.

15
00:00:58,310 --> 00:01:01,530
Compare the shapes: where is each polygon higher?

16
00:01:01,580 --> 00:01:04,530
Higher on the left means more short journeys.

17
00:01:04,580 --> 00:01:07,620
Do not join the ends down to the axis.

18
00:01:07,670 --> 00:01:08,950
Here is the method.

19
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:11,820
Step 1: find each class midpoint.

20
00:01:11,870 --> 00:01:15,570
Step 2: plot each frequency at its midpoint.

21
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:19,290
Step 3: join the points with straight lines.

22
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:23,390
Step 4: for two groups, use the same axes, and a key.

23
00:01:23,440 --> 00:01:25,630
How many pupils are in 9A?

24
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:30,570
Add the frequencies: 4 plus 12 plus 9 plus 3 is 28.

25
00:01:30,620 --> 00:01:34,470
Who has more pupils taking 30 minutes or more?

26
00:01:34,520 --> 00:01:36,420
9A has 3, and 9B has 2.

27
00:01:36,470 --> 00:01:37,480
So 9A.

28
00:01:37,530 --> 00:01:38,200
Your turn.

29
00:01:38,250 --> 00:01:40,070
How many pupils are in 9B?

30
00:01:40,120 --> 00:01:43,050
Pause and work it out.

31
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:47,200
9 plus 11 plus 6 plus 2 is also 28.

32
00:01:47,250 --> 00:01:55,420
For discrete data, like goals in a match grouped 0 to 2, 3 to 5 and 6 to 8, plot at 1, 4 and 7.

33
00:01:55,470 --> 00:01:59,020
Rob plots the class 10 up to 20, frequency 12.

34
00:01:59,070 --> 00:02:01,130
Which line is wrong?

35
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:02,300
Line 1 is wrong.

36
00:02:02,350 --> 00:02:04,820
He used the class boundary.

37
00:02:04,870 --> 00:02:08,100
Plot at the midpoint: 15, 12.

38
00:02:08,150 --> 00:02:16,200
Health services compare the ages of patients in two years, or two towns, with frequency polygons on one graph.

39
00:02:16,250 --> 00:02:17,520
One last question.

40
00:02:17,570 --> 00:02:21,330
Which class has shorter journeys overall: 9B, or 9A?

41
00:02:21,380 --> 00:02:24,700
Pause the video and choose.

42
00:02:24,750 --> 00:02:24,910
9B.

43
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:29,680
Its polygon is higher on the left, where the times are short.

44
00:02:29,730 --> 00:02:34,000
To remember: plot the frequency at the class midpoint.

45
00:02:34,050 --> 00:02:36,480
Join with straight lines.

46
00:02:36,530 --> 00:02:39,270
Great for comparing two groups.

47
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:41,870
Use the same axes, and a key.

48
00:02:41,920 --> 00:02:47,270
Great work! Now practise lesson 15.1 on Camday.
